0.02501751109957695, 0.005302485544234514, 0.014476425014436245, -0.00689323153346777, -0.04367204010486603, 0.016162998974323273]], "input": ["# Antipsychotics and Hyperprolactinemia Community\n\nThis community focuses on the interrelated entities of antipsychotics, prolactin (PRL), and hyperprolactinemia, highlighting the medical implications of these relationships. Antipsychotics are known to influence prolactin levels, leading to conditions such as hyperprolactinemia and macroprolactinoma, which are significant in understanding hormonal health and psychiatric treatment.\n\n## Antipsychotics as a common cause of hyperprolactinemia\n\nAntipsychotics are frequently linked to hyperprolactinemia due to their effects on dopamine receptors, which play a crucial role in regulating prolactin secretion. This relationship is particularly notable with first-generation antipsychotics, which are known to cause marked increases in prolactin levels. The implications of this are significant, as elevated prolactin can lead to various health issues, including reproductive health problems and hormonal imbalances. [Data: Entities (3908), Relationships (12706, 12707)]\n\n## Prolactin's role in reproductive health\n\nProlactin (PRL) is essential for lactation and has various roles in reproductive health, metabolism, and immune regulation. Elevated levels of prolactin can indicate underlying health issues, such as pituitary tumors or hormonal disorders. Understanding the role of prolactin is crucial for managing conditions like hyperprolactinemia, which can arise from multiple factors, including medication use and pituitary adenomas. [Data: Entities (3740), Relationships (11839, 12424)]\n\n## Hyperprolactinemia as a multifactorial condition\n\nHyperprolactinemia is characterized by elevated prolactin levels and can result from various causes, including medications, pituitary tumors, and other physiological conditions. The complexity of this condition necessitates careful evaluation to 